research/audit/wave6-refuter-tasks/cex-limsup-infinite-not-attained-in-r.md-2654-[L2] Index arithmetic, first half: $j \ge N$ implies $j + K \ge N + K$, and $N + K \ge N$ ([[def-nat-order]], [[lem-nat-order-add-compatible]]).
research/audit/wave6-refuter-tasks/cex-limsup-infinite-not-attained-in-r.md-2655-
research/audit/wave6-refuter-tasks/cex-limsup-infinite-not-attained-in-r.md-2656-[L3] Index arithmetic, second half: every $k \ge N + K$ has the form $k = j + K$ with $j \ge N$. Indeed $N + K \le k$ gives $i$ with $(N+K) + i = k$; put $j := N + i$, so that $j \ge N$ and $j + K = (N+i) + K = N + (i + K) = N + (K + i) = (N+K) + i = k$ ([[def-nat-order]], [[lem-nat-add-associative]], [[lem-nat-add-commutative]]).
research/audit/wave6-refuter-tasks/cex-limsup-infinite-not-attained-in-r.md-2657-
research/audit/wave6-refuter-tasks/cex-limsup-infinite-not-attained-in-r.md:2658:[L4] The order on $\mathbb{N}$ is reflexive and transitive, so $k \ge N + K \ge N$ gives $k \ge N$; and $j + 0 = j$ for every $j$ ([[thm-nat-linear-order]], [[def-nat-order]], [[def-nat-addition]]).
