research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-a8r2-konig.prompt.md-888-  interesting invariant here; the least element property is.
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-a8r2-konig.prompt.md-889-- **The model case is $(\mathbb{N}, \le)$**, which is a linear order
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-a8r2-konig.prompt.md:890:  ([[thm-nat-linear-order]]) in which every nonempty subset has a least element
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-a8r2-konig.prompt.md-891-  ([[thm-well-ordering-principle]]). Ordinals, defined later on this page, are
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-a8r2-konig.prompt.md-892-  the exact generalisation of that picture.
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-a8r2-konig.prompt.md-893-- $(\mathbb{Z}, \le)$ and $(\mathbb{R}, \le)$ are total orders but not
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-a8r2-konig.prompt.md-894-  well-orders: $\mathbb{Z}$ has no least element at all, and the bounded set
