research/audit/wave5-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6-049-cor-monotone-converges-iff-bounded.prompt.md-281-$k = 0$: $j \le 0$ forces $j = 0$, and $x_0 \le x_0$. Assume the statement for
research/audit/wave5-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6-049-cor-monotone-converges-iff-bounded.prompt.md-282-$k$ and let $j \le \sigma(k)$. If $j = \sigma(k)$ then $x_j \le x_{\sigma(k)}$ by
research/audit/wave5-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6-049-cor-monotone-converges-iff-bounded.prompt.md-283-reflexivity. Otherwise $j < \sigma(k)$, and then $j \le k$: were $k < j$ we would
research/audit/wave5-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6-049-cor-monotone-converges-iff-bounded.prompt.md-284-have $k < j < \sigma(k)$, which [[lem-nat-discrete]] excludes, so $j \le k$ by
research/audit/wave5-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6-049-cor-monotone-converges-iff-bounded.prompt.md:285:totality of the order on $\mathbb{N}$ ([[thm-nat-linear-order]]). The induction
research/audit/wave5-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6-049-cor-monotone-converges-iff-bounded.prompt.md-286-hypothesis gives $x_j \le x_k$, and $x_k \le x_{\sigma(k)}$ by assumption, so
research/audit/wave5-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6-049-cor-monotone-converges-iff-bounded.prompt.md-287-$x_j \le x_{\sigma(k)}$ by transitivity. This completes the induction. The three
research/audit/wave5-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6-049-cor-monotone-converges-iff-bounded.prompt.md-288-remaining equivalences are the same argument with $\le$ replaced by $<$,
research/audit/wave5-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6-049-cor-monotone-converges-iff-bounded.prompt.md-289-$\ge$ or $>$, transitivity of the strict order being used in the same place.
