research/audit/wave5-dispatch/audit-beta-topology-separation-apply.result.json:16:  "tail": "**Audit-Beta report — wave 5, `topology-separation` batch. A4 applied.**\n\nEverything A3 approved for this batch is on disk. One thing cannot close at A4, and it isn't mine to close.\n\n## Applied\n\n**Pure retag — 72 items, 99 URLs.** All 72 now carry `provenance.statement`/`provenance.proof` matching their ledger row; 99 URLs went into `sources.references` across exactly 60 items, the count §5c approved. Applied by a new idempotent applier (`research/audit/apply-wave5-sep-retag.mjs`) that writes only the provenance block and missing URLs — never `verification:`, the body, or `deps` — so the retag voids no judge stamp. No legacy `authorship:` line existed to delete; no `generation:` block was needed, since no item in this batch is `ai-generated`. It rode the driver's `pre-A4`/`post-A4` snapshots, no snapshots of its own.\n\n**Three material repairs**, each with its own dedicated snapshot, each with its retag folded into the same edit so the item records one transition rather than two:\n\n- `lem-ordinal-order-topology-is-t3` — step 4.1's tag `[step 3.1, L4]` → `[step 3.1, L4, L6]`. The step concludes $B$ is a *closed neighbourhood*, which is exactly what `[L6]` supplies.\n- `ex-the-particular-point-topology-in-the-separation-hierarchy` — `[L3]` deleted. A3 declined my preferred option of tagging it into steps 1.3 and 2.1, and **A3 is right**: I reread both steps and each reads closedness straight off `[A1]`, which lists the closed sets outright. Tagging `L3` there would have manufactured the citation-precision defect this audit exists to find. Last label, so no renumbering; the Remark keeps its own `[[thm-closure-characterisation-top]]` link.\n- `thm-compact-hausdorff-tychonoff-from-the-ultrafilter-lemma` — step 2.1's false codomain repaired to \"defines a point $p\\in\\prod_{i\\in I}X_i$, namely the function $i\\mapsto p_i$\". I had recorded this as nonfatal notation; A3 overrode that and I agree — it's the step carrying why no choice function over $I$ is needed.\n\nAll three lost their stale `verification.judge` and `audited` stamps and now sit in depcheck's `published-unaudited` class, which is the correct A4 output. I certified none of them.\n\n**Ledger:** `escalated_to_alpha: true` on the E1 row. The field was *absent*, not `false` as §3 states. I made it derive from the evidence class rather than hardcoding the id; the diff touches exactly that one field on that one row.\n\n## Gates\n\n`proof-contract.mjs --strict` went from **3 errors to 0** (51/51 items, 372 citations, 339 steps). `citecheck` 72/72 pass, `precheck` 3/3 pass, `reflow` reported all three already canonical, `finite-smoke` and `risk-report` clean, `depcheck --pending-audit-ok` exit 0.\n\n## The blocker\n\n`content-policy.mjs --audit` reports **one error**: `audit-ledger-alpha-concurrence` on `ex-free-ultrafilter-converging-in-a-convergent-sequence-space`. A3 §3 approved these rows with `alpha_concurred: false` and routed concurrence to A6 — but `content-policy.mjs:319` makes that combination a hard error, and `gates.mjs` runs the gate at **A4**. Eight rows wave-wide are affected (my one, plus countability's seven), so the countability Beta will hit the same wall.\n\nI left it as A3 specified rather than resolving it, because none of the three moves is mine: setting `alpha_concurred: true` is self-certifying my own evidence class; falling back to `ai-generated` is mechanically available (nothing depends on this item) but would enter a determination both A3 and I believe false, purely to turn a gate green. The fix I'd recommend is Alpha ruling on the eight concurrences before the A4 gate — the evidence is already assembled and the ruling is a ledger write, not a reading of changed text. Failing that, demote the error at A4 the way `depcheck --pending-audit-ok` already demotes `published-unaudited`, for the identical reason and with A6 keeping it hard.\n\n## One thing outside my scope\n\nSibling Betas ran A4 concurrently against the shared `wave5-touches.json`. `touchlog report --min 2` now names six items as \"repaired more than once\" — **none in this batch**. I make no claim about whether they're real or the wave-2 granularity phantom recurring, but Alpha should treat those counts as unverified rather than as an escalation trigger. Every one of my 72 items records exactly one transition.\n\nFull record with old/new text and derivations is in `research/audit/wave5-topology-separation.findings.md` under \"A4 — applied\"."
