research/audit/wave5-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-a3apply.result.json:16:  "tail": "Both A3 decisions are applied. Gates green.\n\n## Gate output, verbatim\n\n```\nproof-contract: 0 error(s), 0 warning(s), 26/26 item(s) checked\n```\n\n```\nPASS items/ex-nested-intervals-single-point.md (direct)\nPASS items/cex-nested-open-intervals-empty.md (direct)\n\n2 checked, 0 failing — all clean\n```\n\nBare whole-corpus precheck: `2111 checked, 0 failing — all clean`.\n\n## R1 — `ex-nested-intervals-single-point`, `L8` added to step 2.2\n\n**A3 is right and my earlier reading was wrong.** Step 2.2 asserts `0 < n <= j+1` and infers `0 < 1/(j+1) <= 1/n`. `[L4]` states only the strict form, so it does not license a nonstrict conclusion from a nonstrict hypothesis; the missing piece is `a <= b` iff `a < b` or `a = b`, which `def-ordered-field` defines and derives from (O1) Trichotomy — exactly `[L8]`. My addendum's point that `j >= n` gives `j+1 > n` strictly is true but describes an argument the item does not make; the written step routes through `<=`. Three published items (`lem-of-triangle-inequality` `[L2]`, `lem-finite-sum-laws` `[L4]`, `lem-triangle-inequality-finite` `[L4]`) flag this same move in these same terms citing `def-ordered-field`, so the tag is house convention, not padding. This also closes N4 for this item.\n\n## R2 — `cex-nested-open-intervals-empty`, `[L5]` deleted and `L6`→`L5`\n\nApplied in the fact list and in steps 1.1 and 4.1; no label gap.\n\n**`thm-nested-interval-property` stays in `deps`.** The rules neither require nor permit its removal: `depcheck`'s load-bearing scan excludes Remarks by design (\"a 'see also' there is not a dependency\"), so nothing demands the edge; and `depcheck` has no error or warning class for a declared dependency no fact cites, while SCHEMA §3 makes `deps` a lower bound, so nothing forbids it. The choice is dispositional — a `deps` edge drives rendered Prerequisites and the flowchart, so dropping one is a public-interface change owing `impact-audit` closure for no gain, which is the instruction A3 gave for the parallel `lem-uniform-metric-on-a-function-space` case.\n\n## Two things worth your attention\n\n**The dispatch's contract instruction didn't match the file.** There was no `L6` *citation* entry to renumber — old `[L6]` was the refuted claim, a fact with no `[[link]]`, and `proof-contract.mjs` requires a contract only per fact/link pair. The renumbering actually due was in the `derivations` `inputs` for steps 1.1 and 4.1, and that is what I changed. Nothing was dropped. All three `unused_reason` keys are gone.\n\n**I ran `reflow.mts` and did not keep its output.** Neither repair hard-wrapped anything, so reflow found nothing to fix in the facts or steps; its only effect was unwrapping the **Remarks bullets**, which the surrounding published corpus stores wrapped. I restored both files from `HEAD` and re-applied only the licensed edits, so the diff is exactly the two repairs plus the stale-stamp deletions. If you want the corpus reflow-canonical, that's a separate sweep, not a rider on two published items.\n\nStamps and snapshots: both repairs are material, so I deleted each item's stale `verification.judge` (`z-ai/glm-5.2`, 2026-07-26) and obsolete `audited` stamp; A6 certifies, not me. Dedicated pre-edit snapshots `pre-repair-ex-nested-intervals-single-point` and `pre-repair-cex-nested-open-intervals-empty` are in `wave5-touches.json`. `depcheck` now shows 11 `published-unaudited` errors — two mine, nine from sibling batches — the documented A4→A6 window that `--pending-audit-ok` takes to 0 errors.\n\nWrites this stage: the two items, the batch contract, my findings file, and the two snapshot rows. No provenance ledger row; the A1 retag pass is still unapplied."
